// lexdb over TNS: recursion in the Oracle door (design/lexdb-dialect-gaps.md, "Recursion in the doors"), checked against
// Oracle Database Free (gen_oracle.py --check: the answers below are Oracle's): recursive subquery factoring (the column
// list required, ORA-32039; one anchor and one recursive branch, in either order; ORA-32040, 32041, 32486, 32487,
// 32490, 32042, 01790, 32038), Oracle's iteration order where no ORDER BY gives one, its cycle check (ORA-32044: a row
// that is an ancestor's again, a recursive branch without a predicate), SEARCH DEPTH FIRST and BREADTH FIRST BY ...
// [ASC | DESC] [NULLS FIRST | LAST] SET (the ordering column, the rows in its order), CYCLE ... SET ... TO ... DEFAULT
// (the marks, cycle rows not expanded, NULLs; ORA-32047, 32048, 32046, 32482, 32489), recursion under a join, GROUP BY,
// FETCH FIRST, an INSERT and an UPDATE fed by one; hierarchical queries: START WITH, CONNECT BY [NOCYCLE] with PRIOR
// either side and LEVEL, LEVEL, CONNECT_BY_ROOT, SYS_CONNECT_BY_PATH, CONNECT_BY_ISLEAF, CONNECT_BY_ISCYCLE, PRIOR in the
// select list, ORDER SIBLINGS BY (ASC, DESC, a text), WHERE after the hierarchy, GROUP BY LEVEL, LEVEL in CONNECT BY,
// upward (id = PRIOR boss), a derived table, ORA-01436, 01788, 30929; the dual row generator (CONNECT BY LEVEL <= n, <
// n, 10,000 rows). Each statement runs with set lexdb_batch = off (the row engine) and on: "same" and the answer, or
// DIFFERENT and both.
